1. What is an Array in Java?
An array is a fixed-size, indexed data structure that stores elements of the same data type in contiguous memory locations.

int[] arr = new int[5];


Interview one-liner
“An array is a fixed-size, indexed collection of homogeneous elements.”

2. Key Characteristics of Arrays
Fixed size (cannot grow/shrink)
Zero-based index
Stores homogeneous data
Can store primitive or object references
Stored in heap memory
Length is immutable (length variable)

3. Types of Arrays in Java
3.1 One-Dimensional Array
int[] arr = {10, 20, 30};

3.2 Two-Dimensional Array
int[][] matrix = new int[2][3];

3.3 Multi-Dimensional Array
int[][][] cube = new int[2][2][2];

3.4 Jagged Array (Array of Arrays)
int[][] jagged = new int[2][];
jagged[0] = new int[2];
jagged[1] = new int[3];

10. Array Length vs String length()
int[] a = new int[5];
System.out.println(a.length); // property

String s = "Java";
System.out.println(s.length()); // method

11. Arrays Class (java.util.Arrays) 🔥
Common Methods:
Arrays.sort(arr);
Arrays.toString(arr);
Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 20);
Arrays.copyOf(arr, 5);
Arrays.equals(arr1, arr2);

Example
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {30, 10, 20};
        Arrays.sort(arr);

        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}

Output
[10, 20, 30]

12. Copying Arrays
Using loop:
int[] copy = new int[arr.length];
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    copy[i] = arr[i];
}

Using System.arraycopy():
System.arraycopy(arr, 0, copy, 0, arr.length);

Using Arrays.copyOf():
int[] copy = Arrays.copyOf(arr, arr.length);

13. Array of Objects (🔥 Important)
class Employee {
    int id;
    Employee(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee[] emp = new Employee[2];
        emp[0] = new Employee(1);
        emp[1] = new Employee(2);

        for (Employee e : emp) {
            System.out.println(e.id);
        }
    }
}

Output
1
2

14. Arrays vs Collections (🔥 Very Common)
| Feature     | Array       | Collection      |
| ----------- | ----------- | --------------- |
| Size        | Fixed       | Dynamic         |
| Data type   | Homogeneous | Objects only    |
| Performance | Faster      | Slightly slower |
| Generics    | ❌           | ✅            |
| Utilities   | Limited     | Rich API        |

15. Sorting & Searching
Binary Search (Sorted Array)
int index = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 20);

16. Common Exceptions
ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
int[] a = new int[3];
System.out.println(a[5]);

NullPointerException
int[] a = null;
System.out.println(a.length);

17. Arrays & Performance

Access → O(1)
Search (linear) → O(n)
Binary search → O(log n) (sorted)
Memory efficient
No resizing cost
